// probe_api_name.nvgt - what does midi_api_name() actually return on windows?
//
// This exists because e2e_winmm.nvgt reported this, once and only once the
// engine would compile the script at all:
//
//     E2E_OK LOADED `|�>   $    fH�
//     E2E_FAIL expected a windows midi backend, got `|�>   $    fH�
//
// midi_api_name() is not built out of script memory. It is
//
//     return "nvmidi/" + RtMidiIn::getApiDisplayName(apis[0]);
//
// and getApiDisplayName returns rtmidi_api_names[api][1] - a pointer to a
// string literal in an `extern "C"` table defined in RtMidi.cpp - through a
// std::string return value. That path was measured locally first, outside this
// job, with a probe compiled against the same RtMidi.cpp the plugin links:
//
//     compiled_count=1
//     api[0] enum=5 table0=dummy table1=Dummy disp.size=5
//     disp.hex=44 75 6d 6d 79   disp.raw=[Dummy]
//
// so the table and the string are correct in the object file, and whatever
// mangles them on windows happens between that object file and the script
// rather than inside it.
//
// What is being separated here
// ---------------------------
//
// A literal written in this file, a string built inside the plugin, and a
// string handed out by RtMidi's object file. If the literal survives and the
// other two do not, the fault is in how the plugin's code calls into its own
// RtMidi and is nothing to do with nvgt. If all three are garbage, the string
// type itself does not cross the boundary and every string-returning call in
// this plugin - get_port_name, to_string, midi_last_error - is affected.
//
// The title literal is deliberate: it is a byte-for-byte control that cannot
// be affected by anything RtMidi does. Absent from the output, this script did
// not run; present and intact, the engine's own strings are fine.

#pragma plugin nvmidi

// `out` is a reserved keyword in this engine - "ERROR: Instead found reserved
// keyword 'out'" at the declaration, measured on the runner, not guessed. It
// cost this probe two runs that looked like plugin failures and were compile
// errors, which is why the accumulator below is named `hex` and not `out`.
string hex_of(const string&in s) {
	string hex = "";
	const string digits = "0123456789abcdef";
	for (uint i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
		// A byte cannot be taken from a string in this engine - indexing
		// yields a one character string, and a character cannot be treated
		// as a number ("Can't implicitly convert from 'const string' to
		// 'uint'", measured). So the index of the character in a digit
		// table is the value, which is a find() and therefore parses.
		const string ch = s.substr(i, 1);
		const int hi = digits.find(ch);
		if (hi < 0) { hex += "?"; continue; }
		hex += digits.substr(hi, 1);
	}
	return hex;
}

void main() {
	print("PROBE_BEGIN");
	// Nothing between this line and PROBE_END may read the string's length,
	// walk its characters, or copy it into another string.
	//
	// Measured, on the runner, twice. A probe that did all three died with
	// exit code -1073740791 (0xC0000409, STATUS_STACK_BUFFER_OVERRUN) before
	// its second print, and the e2e script - which concatenates the same
	// value into "E2E_OK LOADED " + api and then stops - survived that one
	// concatenation and produced garbage on both sides of it. A value whose
	// length is not a length explains both: heap reads run off the end of the
	// buffer and the copy writes as much as it was told.
	//
	// So the value is printed raw and alone. print() of a single string does
	// not consult the length in this engine - the e2e script prints "E2E_BEGIN"
	// and other bare literals with no arithmetic anywhere - and if that is
	// wrong too, the crash moves up one line and says so.
	// A second string-returning call, registered with the same convention and
	// no string arithmetic of its own, as a control against midi_api_name.
	//
	// The two differ in exactly one way: midi_api_name builds its result as
	// `"nvmidi/" + RtMidiIn::getApiDisplayName(...)`, so the value crosses the
	// boundary as a temporary produced by operator+. midi_output_port_name
	// returns a std::string that came straight out of RtMidi with no expression
	// involved. If the control survives and midi_api_name does not, the fault
	// is in how the result is built; if both are equally broken, the fault is
	// in how a string return is declared to the engine, which is a change in
	// nvmidi.cpp beside the registration and not in any script.
	// One call, not two. The second one - midi_output_port_name(9999) - was
	// added to separate "RtMidi handed back garbage" from "out of range", and
	// the run answered the first half before it got there: printed from inside
	// the plugin, port 0 came back as a correct 30 byte name. Repeating it
	// costs a run and a crash for a question already settled.
	print("PROBE_OTHER_BEGIN");
	print(midi_output_port_name(0));
	print("PROBE_OTHER_END");
	// The same value, from the same plugin, from the same port, in the same
	// run - the only difference is the return type the plugin is registered
	// with. Whatever this prints, the pair of lines above and below it is the
	// measurement.
	print("PROBE_CSTR_BEGIN");
	print(midi_output_port_name_cstr(0));
	print("PROBE_CSTR_END");

	string api = midi_api_name();
	print(api);
	// `api` is deliberately never mentioned again below this point.
	print("PROBE_END");
	// nvgt_plugin_version() was tried here and is not a symbol this engine has
	// - "ERROR: No matching symbol 'nvgt_plugin_version'", measured on the
	// runner, which cost the probe a third run. The engine's export list is
	// its own, and the api name is what this file was written to read.
}
